    "id": 81,
    "category": "GenAI",
    "title": "ML Q&A: AI, ML, Deep Learning, and Generative AI foundations",
    "tags": [
      "Artificial Intelligence",
      "Machine Learning",
      "Deep Learning",
      "Generative AI"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Foundations Q&A clarifying AI vs ML vs DL vs GenAI with precise production implications. (~3-minute read, documentation-backed).",
    "sourceUrl": "https://learnhub.local/genai/qa-ml-foundations",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-23T12:26:28.576Z",
    "level": "Beginner",
    "content": "## Scope\nStandard ML taxonomy used across textbooks and vendor docs (no hype definitions).\n\n\n## Visual overview\nVisual summary for **ML Q&A: AI, ML, Deep Learning, and Generative AI foundations** — aligned to this tutorial’s tags and checklist style.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[GenAI topic] --> B[Learn concepts]\n  B --> C[Apply controls]\n  C --> D[Verify with evidence]\n  D --> E[Document + mentor]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((ML Q&A: AI, ML, Deep Learning, and Generat))\n    Artificial Intelligence\n    Machine Learning\n    Deep Learning\n    Generative AI\n    Practice\n      Checklist\n      Failure modes\n```\n\n\n### Q1. Define Artificial Intelligence vs Machine Learning.\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nAI is the broad field of systems that perform tasks associated with human cognition. ML is the subset that learns patterns from data rather than only hand-coded rules.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q2. What is Deep Learning?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nML using multi-layer neural networks that learn hierarchical representations (vision, speech, NLP). Higher data/compute needs; strong when features are hard to hand-engineer.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q3. What is Generative AI?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nModels that generate new content (text, images, code, audio) typically via large neural models (often Transformers). Distinct from purely discriminative classifiers, though hybrids exist.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q4. When is classical ML still the right call?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nTabular clinical/claims risk scores, forecasting with strong features, constrained latency/cost, or when explainability and stable monitoring matter more than open-ended generation.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.### Q5. Architect takeaway?\n\n**Answer (documentation-aligned)**\n\nStart from the decision and constraints. GenAI is one tool in the AI stack—not the default for every prediction problem.\n\n**How to explain this in an interview (30–40 seconds)**\nState the definition, name one official/primary source, give one production control (eval, ACL, timeout, or rollback), and end with a failure mode you would monitor. That structure signals Architect-level judgment—not tool-name recall.\n\n*Production note:* Write the control, the metric, and the rollback before you widen traffic. Prefer evidence (eval + traces) over anecdote when promoting changes.\n\n## Self-check\nGive one example workload for rules, classical ML, DL classifier, and GenAI RAG.\n\n## Documentation deep dive (free public sources)\nAI ⊃ ML ⊃ Deep Learning; Generative AI is the content-producing subset often powered by large Transformers. Classical ML remains best for many tabular clinical/claims problems with tight latency and explainability needs. Pair problem class to stack (rules vs classical ML vs RAG vs agents) before buying GPUs.\n\n**Why this matters in production**\nExecutives say 'AI' when they mean a chatbot, a classifier, or a forecasting model — Architects must translate.\n\n**Implementation checklist**\n- Write the decision and metric before picking GenAI\n- Compare rules vs classical ML vs RAG vs agents explicitly\n- Estimate data readiness and labeling cost\n- Define human oversight level up front\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Defaulting every request to an LLM\n- Ignoring calibration and slice metrics on classifiers\n\n**Architect decision tip**\nIf you cannot explain the control, the metric, and the rollback to a non-engineer stakeholder, the design is not ready for production promotion.\n\n## Worked scenario\nImagine a stakeholder asks for “an AI assistant on our data next sprint.” Using this topic, you would: (1) reframe to a decision + metric + risk tier, (2) choose the thinnest architecture that can hit the metric, (3) require an offline eval set before any pilot, (4) instrument traces with redaction, and (5) define human escalation for unsafe outputs. If any of those five are missing, you are running a demo—not a production GenAI capability.\n\n## 3-minute study path\n1. Skim each Q&A answer once (definitions).\n2. Read this deep dive and open one primary official link from the references.\n3. Practice the interview structure aloud for two questions.\n4. Write one prevention rule for your team wiki from the mistakes list.",
    "pipeline": true,
