    "content": "## Core idea\nWriting tests in Playwright shows up constantly in production work. This lesson turns the official docs outline into a practical mental model you can reuse in interviews and day-to-day coding.\n\nContext signal from docs: Introduction\n\n\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAligned with [Playwright Locators](https://playwright.dev/docs/locators): role-first, then test-id contracts, CSS/XPath last.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Need[Need element] --> Prefer[Prefer user-facing]\n  Prefer --> Role[getByRole + name]\n  Prefer --> Text[getByText / Label / Placeholder]\n  Prefer --> Tid[getByTestId contract]\n  Prefer -->|last resort| Css[locator CSS/XPath]\n  Role --> Act[Click / fill / assert]\n  Text --> Act\n  Tid --> Act\n  Css --> Act\n  Act --> Wait[Auto-wait + retry]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Playwright locators))\n    Recommended\n      getByRole\n      getByText\n      getByLabel\n      getByPlaceholder\n      getByAltText\n      getByTitle\n      getByTestId\n    Engine\n      Auto-wait\n      Retry\n      Strict mode\n    Avoid first\n      Brittle CSS\n      Absolute XPath\n```\n\n\n## Practical rules\n- Build intuition around: Introduction​\n- Build intuition around: First test​\n- Build intuition around: Actions​\n- Build intuition around: Navigation​\n- Build intuition around: Interactions​\n- Prefer clear names and small examples over memorizing every API edge case.\n- Verify behavior with a tiny snippet before wiring it into a large suite.\n\n## Try this\n```js\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';test('has title', async ({ page }) => { await page.goto('https://playwright.dev/'); // Expect a title \"to contain\" a substring. await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Playwright/);});test('get started link', async ({ page }) => { await pag\n```\n\n## Quick quiz\nQ: What is the first thing you should clarify about Writing tests in Playwright?\nA: The exact problem it solves and the smallest example that proves you understand it.\n\n## Key takeaway\nMaster Writing tests in Playwright by explaining it out loud, writing one minimal example, and only then reading deeper docs sections.\n\nFurther reading: https://playwright.dev/docs/writing-tests\n",
