    "level": "beginner",
    "content": "## How to Use beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks Quickly?\n\nThe quickest way to implement hooks in Playwright is to define them within your test suite. Hooks can be set up to run before and after each test or suite, helping to streamline repetitive tasks.\n\nHere's a simple working example using all four hooks:\n\n```js\nconst { test } = require('@playwright/test');\n\nlet browser;\n\ntest.beforeAll(async () => {\n    browser = await playwright.chromium.launch();\n});\n\ntest.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.goto('http://example.com');\n});\n\ntest('should display the title', async ({ page }) => {\n    const title = await page.title();\n    expect(title).toBe('Example Domain');\n});\n\ntest.afterEach(async ({ page }) => {\n    // Cleanup actions like closing cookies or session data\n});\n\ntest.afterAll(async () => {\n    await browser.close();\n});\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Visual overview\nFrom [Fixtures](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-fixtures): declare deps in test args — runner isolates setup/teardown.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Worker[Worker process] --> WFix[Worker fixtures]\n  WFix --> File[Test file]\n  File --> TFix[Test fixtures page/context/request]\n  TFix --> Body[Test body]\n  Body --> Down[Fixture teardown]\n  Hook[beforeEach / afterEach] --> Body\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Fixtures))\n    Built-in\n      page\n      context\n      browser\n      request\n    Custom\n      test.extend\n      auto fixtures\n    Hooks\n      beforeEach\n      afterEach\n      beforeAll\n      afterAll\n    Isolation\n      Per-test context\n      Worker reuse\n```\n\n\n## How to beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks in Detail?\n\nThe hooks in Playwright are designed to manage setup and teardown processes around your tests effectively. The **beforeAll** hook runs once before all tests in a suite, useful for initializing resources that are expensive to create. The **beforeEach** hook runs before each test, ensuring a clean state for every test execution.\n\nAfter tests execute, **afterEach** can clean up resources. Finally, **afterAll** runs once after all tests in a suite, typically used for closing database connections or other global resources.\n\nHere's a more detailed example that illustrates the flow:\n\n```js\nconst { test } = require('@playwright/test');\n\nlet server;\n\ntest.beforeAll(async () => {\n    server = await startTestServer(); // Assume this starts a server\n});\n\ntest.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.goto('http://localhost:3000');\n});\n\ntest('test 1', async ({ page }) => {\n    expect(await page.title()).toBe('Home');\n});\n\ntest('test 2', async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.click('text=About');\n    expect(await page.title()).toBe('About Us');\n});\n\ntest.afterEach(async () => {\n    // Reset any mock states\n});\n\ntest.afterAll(async () => {\n    await server.close(); // Clean up server after all tests\n});\n```\n\nThis code initializes a server before all tests and navigates to a specific page before each test, ensuring that each test starts from a known state. After tests, any mock states are reset, and finally, the server is closed after all tests.\n\n## What is beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks?\n\nThe **beforeEach**, **afterEach**, **beforeAll**, and **afterAll** hooks are part of the test lifecycle methods provided by Playwright. These hooks allow developers to run specific code before or after tests or test suites, making it easier to manage setups and teardowns.\n\nA significant reason for their existence is to reduce code duplication. Instead of repeating setup code in each test, you can centralize it in these hooks. This not only improves code maintainability but also enhances readability.\n\nInternally, when tests are executed, Playwright will handle these hooks in the order they are defined, ensuring that the execution flow is predictable. Introduced in the early versions of Playwright, these hooks align with common practices in modern testing frameworks.\n\nUnderstanding how these hooks interact can significantly impact test outcomes and debugging processes, especially when managing shared state or resources across tests.\n\n## When to Use beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks?\n\n- **Database Setup**: Use **beforeAll** to connect to a database only once for all tests to improve performance.\n- **Page Navigation**: Use **beforeEach** to navigate to a specific page before every test to ensure consistency.\n- **State Reset**: Use **afterEach** to reset application state or clear cookies after each test.\n- **Resource Cleanup**: Use **afterAll** to close connections or free up resources after all tests finish.\n- **Mocking Services**: Implement **beforeEach** to set up mock responses for APIs before each test.\n- **Performance Monitoring**: Use **beforeAll** to start performance monitoring tools for the entire suite of tests.\n\n### Is beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks different from [related concept]?\n\nYes, these hooks differ from one-time setup methods. While **beforeAll** runs once before all tests, one-time setup methods in other frameworks may not support teardown actions post-test execution.\n\n### Does beforeEach afterEach beforeAll afterAll Hooks trigger events?\n\nYes, they can trigger events indirectly by encapsulating logic that emits events during the setup or teardown processes. For instance, they can be used to log information or notify other systems about test states.\n\n## What is the Difference Between [Method A] and [Method B]?\n\n**beforeEach** and **beforeAll** serve different purposes. While **beforeEach** runs before every individual test, **beforeAll** runs only once for the entire test suite. This distinction impacts performance and resource usage significantly.\n\n| Method        | When to Use                                       | Key Behavior                                   | Speed                       |\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|\n| beforeEach    | For setting up state or navigation before each test | Runs for every test case                           | Slower due to multiple calls |\n| afterEach     | For cleaning up state after each test              | Runs for every test case                           | Slower due to multiple calls |\n| beforeAll     | For one-time setup before all tests                | Runs once before the test suite                   | Faster, only executed once   |\n| afterAll      | For one-time cleanup after all tests               | Runs once after the test suite                    | Faster, only executed once   |\n\n## Step by Step Guide with Examples\n\n### Step 1: Set Up Your Test Environment\n\nStart by initializing your Playwright testing environment. Install Playwright if you haven???t already.\n\n```bash\nnpm install @playwright/test\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create a Basic Test File\n\nCreate a file named `example.spec.js` where you will implement your tests.\n\n```js\nconst { test } = require('@playwright/test');\n\ntest('basic test', async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.goto('http://example.com');\n});\n```\n\n### Step 3: Implement beforeAll Hook\n\nUse the **beforeAll** hook to set up global resources or configurations.\n\n```js\nconst { test } = require('@playwright/test');\n\nlet browser;\n\ntest.beforeAll(async () => {\n    browser = await playwright.chromium.launch();\n",
