    "id": 31,
    "category": "Playwright",
    "title": "Page Navigation and URL Control Guide with Examples",
    "tags": [
      "playwright",
      "page navigation",
      "url control",
      "web automation",
      "testing",
      "javascript"
    ],
    "excerpt": "In this comprehensive guide, you'll learn the fundamentals of page navigation and URL control using Playwright. We???ll cover key concepts, practical examples, and best practices to empower your web automation skills.",
    "sourceUrl": "",
    "createdAt": "2026-04-03T10:08:48.941Z",
    "level": "beginner",
    "content": "## How to Use Page Navigation and URL Control Quickly?\n\nPage navigation and URL control are essential for web automation. You can quickly navigate between pages and control URLs in your automation scripts.\n\nHere's a simple example to start with:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://example.com'); // Navigating to a URL\n  await page.click('text=More information...'); // Clicking a link\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Visual overview\nAligned with [Playwright Locators](https://playwright.dev/docs/locators): role-first, then test-id contracts, CSS/XPath last.\n\n### Process flowchart\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Need[Need element] --> Prefer[Prefer user-facing]\n  Prefer --> Role[getByRole + name]\n  Prefer --> Text[getByText / Label / Placeholder]\n  Prefer --> Tid[getByTestId contract]\n  Prefer -->|last resort| Css[locator CSS/XPath]\n  Role --> Act[Click / fill / assert]\n  Text --> Act\n  Tid --> Act\n  Css --> Act\n  Act --> Wait[Auto-wait + retry]\n```\n\n### Topic mindmap\n```mermaid\nmindmap\n  root((Playwright locators))\n    Recommended\n      getByRole\n      getByText\n      getByLabel\n      getByPlaceholder\n      getByAltText\n      getByTitle\n      getByTestId\n    Engine\n      Auto-wait\n      Retry\n      Strict mode\n    Avoid first\n      Brittle CSS\n      Absolute XPath\n```\n\n\n## How to Page Navigation and URL Control in Detail?\n\nUnderstanding page navigation involves grasping how web pages load and how we can interact with them. In Playwright, the `goto()` method allows you to navigate to a specific URL.\n\nWhen you call `await page.goto(url)`, Playwright makes a request to the specified URL. The page loads, and you can interact with it as if you were a user. After loading, you can perform actions like clicking links or filling out forms.\n\nHere???s a detailed example:\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://example.com'); // Navigate to example.com\n\n  // Wait for a specific element to load\n  await page.waitForSelector('text=More information...');\n  await page.click('text=More information...'); // Click on a link\n\n  // Wait for the new page to load\n  await page.waitForURL('https://example.com/more-info');\n  console.log('Navigated to:', page.url());\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\nIn this example, we navigate to `https://example.com`, wait for a link to appear, click it, and wait for the new URL to load before logging the current URL.\n\n## What is Page Navigation and URL Control?\n\n**Page navigation** refers to the ability to move between different pages on the web, while **URL control** involves managing the URLs you navigate to. These concepts are vital for web testing and automation.\n\nIn web automation, controlling page navigation is crucial. When you visit a website, you interact with different pages for various tasks, like filling forms or checking information. The navigation process must be seamless for effective automation.\n\nPage navigation exists to simulate human browsing behavior. By using automation tools like Playwright, developers can ensure their applications function correctly across various browser environments.\n\nThe introduction of Playwright has made it easier to navigate pages programmatically. It supports multiple browsers and provides a robust API to control page interactions. This flexibility allows developers to write tests that mimic user actions accurately.\n\n## When to Use Page Navigation and URL Control?\n\n- **Testing Web Applications:** Validate that links lead to the correct pages and behave as expected.\n- **Scraping Data:** Collect information from multiple pages by navigating through them.\n- **Automated User Actions:** Simulate user behavior for tasks like form submissions and data entry.\n- **Monitoring Website Changes:** Check if content on specific pages updates correctly over time.\n- **Multi-Page User Flows:** Ensure that user journeys across multiple pages function correctly.\n\n### Is Page Navigation and URL Control different from Browser Contexts?\n\nYes, page navigation deals specifically with moving between pages within a browser, while browser contexts are isolated environments for running tests. You can have multiple contexts and navigate different pages within each context.\n\n### Does Page Navigation and URL Control trigger events?\n\nYes, navigating to a new page can trigger events like `load`, `domcontentloaded`, and `networkidle`. These events can be useful for waiting until the page is fully loaded before performing actions.\n\n## What is the Difference Between `page.goto()` and `page.click()`?\n\n`page.goto()` is used to navigate directly to a specified URL, whereas `page.click()` simulates a user clicking on an element, which may lead to a navigation event.\n\n| Method         | When to Use                 | Key Behavior                          | Speed       |\n|----------------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------|-------------|\n| `page.goto()`  | Direct URL navigation       | Navigates immediately to a URL      | Fast        |\n| `page.click()` | Clicking on a link/button    | Triggers navigation based on actions | May vary    |\n| `page.fill()`  | Filling inputs              | Changes form inputs                  | Fast        |\n| `page.type()`  | Typing into inputs          | Simulates user typing                 | Slower      |\n\n## Step by Step Guide with Examples\n\n### Step 1: Launch the Browser\n\nThe first step is to launch the Playwright browser.\n\n```js\nconst { chromium } = require('playwright');\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch(); // Launching the browser\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\n})();\n```\n\n### Step 2: Navigate to a URL\n\nNext, you need to navigate to the desired URL using the `goto()` method.\n\n```js\nawait page.goto('https://example.com'); // Navigating to example.com\n```\n\n### Step 3: Wait for an Element\n\nBefore interacting with the page, wait for a specific element to load.\n\n```js\nawait page.waitForSelector('text=More information...'); // Waiting for the link\n```\n\n### Step 4: Click a Link\n\nAfter the element is loaded, click it to navigate to another page.\n\n```js\nawait page.click('text=More information...'); // Clicking the link\n```\n\n### Step 5: Verify the URL\n\nOnce on the new page, verify that the URL has changed as expected.\n\n```js\nconsole.log('Current URL:', page.url()); // Logging the current URL\n```\n\n### Step 6: Close the Browser\n\nFinally, close the browser to end the session.\n\n```js\nawait browser.close(); // Closing the browser\n})();\n```\n\n## Common Patterns and Variations\n\n### Navigating with URL Parameters\n\nYou can navigate to a URL with parameters by appending them directly.\n\n```js\nawait page.goto('https://example.com/search?query=playwright'); // URL with parameters\n```\n\n### Handling Navigation Events\n\nUse event listeners to handle navigation events effectively.\n\n```js\npage.on('load', () => console.log('Page loaded!')); // Listening for load event\nawait page.goto('https://example.com');\n```\n\n### Using `waitForNavigation`\n\nTo wait for navigation after an action, use `waitForNavigation()` method.\n\n```js\nawait Promise.all([\n  page.click('text=More information...\n",
